    "slug": "vlm-geolocation-research",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: 'Multimodal Image Geolocation (Geo-HiPO)'\npublishedAt: '2025-08-15'\ndateRange: 'May 2025 - Aug 2025'\nsummary: 'Trained Vision-Language Models to predict where photos were taken by teaching models physical distance instead of classification, running experiments across multi-node H100 and V100 GPU clusters.'\ntechStack: 'Python, PyTorch, CUDA, Qwen2.5-VL, Kimi-VL, Hugging Face Transformers, TRL, PEFT, LoRA, DPO, SimPO, SLURM, vLLM, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention-2, Docker, Linux, OpenCV, Pillow, Albumentations, NumPy, pandas, GeoNames, OpenStreetMap'\n---\n\n## The Problem\n\nMost geolocation models treat picking a location like a multiple-choice test:\n\n* If a photo was taken in Paris, guessing Versailles is graded as completely wrong, the same as guessing Tokyo.\n* In reality, Versailles is only 12 miles away, while Tokyo is over 5,000 miles away.\n\nDuring my research at the University of Central Florida Center for Research in Computer Vision (May 2025 - Aug 2025), I worked on Geo-HiPO. The goal was to teach vision-language models how close a guess is in the real world based on actual distance, rather than just marking guesses as right or wrong.\n\n---\n\n## How It Works\n\nWe used preference optimization (DPO) to rank model guesses by physical distance:\n\n* **Generate guesses**: The model predicts multiple possible locations for an image.\n* **Check distance**: We turn each predicted place name into GPS coordinates and calculate how far it is from the real location.\n* **Rank the guesses**: The closer guess becomes the preferred answer, and the farther guess becomes the rejected answer.\n* **Step-by-step training**: The model first learns broad areas like continents and countries before training on specific cities.\n\n<ResearchPoster />\n\n---\n\n## What I Built\n\n* **30M+ image pipeline**: Built multiprocessing data loaders in Python to clean and process over 30 million images, saving over 100 hours of pipeline runtime.\n* **Geocoding validation**: Set up automated checks with GeoNames and OpenStreetMap to filter out bad coordinates and fake place names before training.\n* **Cluster job management**: Managed training and testing across multi-node NVIDIA H100 and V100 GPU clusters using SLURM, running sweeps of up to 50 jobs at once.\n* **Fast batch inference**: Used vLLM to run high-speed batched inference on Qwen2.5-VL and Kimi-VL models to generate test predictions quickly.\n* **Memory optimization**: Set up FlashAttention-2, mixed precision (AMP), and DeepSpeed so large vision models could fit on GPUs without crashing.\n* **Debugging cluster issues**: Resolved GCC and CUDA version conflicts on cluster nodes and fixed token padding bugs between image features and text.\n\n---\n\n## Results\n\nTested on the standard IM2GPS3K benchmark:\n\n* **Qwen2.5-VL-7B with HiPO** reached **35.87% accuracy within 25 km** and **54.59% within 250 km**, beating the base model (34.13% and 52.22%).\n* Standard fine-tuning actually lowered accuracy to 33.97%, proving that teaching models with physical distance works much better than simple label matching.\n\n---\n\n## Technologies Used\n\n* **Vision-Language Models**: Qwen2.5-VL (3B, 7B, 32B, 72B), Kimi-VL\n* **Deep Learning Frameworks**: PyTorch, Hugging Face Transformers, Hugging Face Datasets, Hugging Face Accelerate, TRL, PEFT, LoRA\n* **Optimization & Alignment**: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), SimPO, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Curriculum Learning\n* **ML Acceleration & Runtimes**: vLLM, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention-2, PyTorch AMP, bitsandbytes, FP8, NF4, INT8 Quantization\n* **HPC & Cluster Infrastructure**: SLURM Workload Manager, NVIDIA H100 GPUs, NVIDIA V100 GPUs, Multi-Node Clusters, CUDA, GCC\n* **Computer Vision & Image Processing**: OpenCV, Pillow (PIL), Albumentations, Scene Understanding, Visual Reasoning\n* **Data Engineering & Geocoding**: Python Multiprocessing, Custom Data Loaders, Haversine Distance, GeoNames, OpenStreetMap (OSM), NumPy, pandas\n* **Systems & Tooling**: Linux (Ubuntu, RHEL), Docker, Conda, Git, Bash\n"
